00:00:06.188 --> 00:00:10.050
JUSTIN BROUGHTON: All
right, are we ready?

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.340
Yes?

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:11.910
Yes?

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:12.410
All right.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:13.980
Testing Games in the Cloud.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.190
Welcome, I am Justin.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.140
I'm an engineering
manager at Google.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:23.070
And today, we're going
to talk about testing

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.040
mobile games in the Cloud.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.960
So hopefully, all
of you have seen

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.140
the what we're
calling a Dory, which

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.000
was on the previous slide.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.030
Also, there's a link
in the Session page

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.340
for this session in the app.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.123
And with that, you
can ask questions

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:41.790
if you haven't been
to a session before.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.920
You can actually ask questions.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.470
During this presentation,
you can ask questions there,

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:51.310
and we will have plenty of
time for questions at the end.

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:55.290
In fact, I'd even like to
open up to a little bit more

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.640
than perhaps questions.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.780
So maybe, understand a little
bit more about your experiences

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.180
with testing of games.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.050
But we'll get there.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.740
So let's start by asking
the question, when

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:18.800
we test apps and games,
what are we looking for?

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:25.370
Well in some respects, games
are just like applications.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:29.360
So we're looking
for signals that

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.700
might indicate a problem
or a bad experience,

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.690
such as slow startup time.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:42.500
Of course, we're also
looking for crashes

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.310
or not responding events,
which give a particularly

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.110
bad experience to the user.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:57.210
We're also looking for
incorrect behavior, where

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.530
the game does not
behave as we intend

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.195
or indeed as the user expects.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:12.690
But with games, we
care very specifically

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.060
about a number of things.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:19.040
One of these is incorrect
or slow rendering.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.960
That is because games provide
an immersive experience--

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.110
an immersive experience
which is thoroughly ruined

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:29.400
if your UI is janky or slow.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:38.010
So what are games companies
doing today around testing?

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.410
Well, I've talked with
perhaps many of you

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:47.570
and certainly a lot
of games companies.

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.900
And there's a whole hell of a
lot of manual testing going on.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:57.290
That is, manual testing
before you release,

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.420
and then releasing
to a whole bunch

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.500
of alpha or beta customers
for a bit of free testing.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.570
There is some-- not a lot--
there is some test automation.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.340
But it's usually
homegrown, which

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.460
means it's specific to the
perhaps proprietary game

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.950
engine you're using,
or to the company

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.900
that you've built it in.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.150
So it's not widely applicable.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.200
It's not widely applicable
outside of the company in which

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.308
you work or that game engine.

00:03:33.308 --> 00:03:35.350
Well, you might be asking,
why is this a problem?

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:43.120
Well, it's a problem
mostly because of expense.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:49.560
You can imagine training
all of those testers

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.910
to not only play the
game, but actually

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:54.410
to become good at
the game-- so they

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:56.810
can get to the later level,
so they can find the issues

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.930
that your users are going to
find some months after you've

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:03.400
released it into production.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.260
And then, you have to
manually test across

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.710
a variety of different devices,
and then verify the results--

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:15.450
which, of course, is
even more expensive.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:21.850
And the testing has to be
repeated every time you

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:24.072
want to release to production.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.710
The testing-- and
this is probably

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:32.620
the key part-- the testing is
also performed particularly

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:36.490
late in the process, which
means you've no idea which

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:41.280
of the myriad of changes
your developers have

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.320
made that could be
possibly causing

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.110
this particular problem.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.123
So catching problems earlier
is just not possible.

00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:53.040
And we all know that
catching problems earlier

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.290
makes them easier to
fix, as well as cheaper.

00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:01.640
And lastly, there is
no safety net, which

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:05.400
means your game's developers,
or you as a game developer,

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.940
you don't know if the change
you're about to commit

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.940
is going to break the game
in some fundamental way.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.540
So why are games
companies and developers

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.100
not flocking to
automated testing?

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:32.340
Well, it turns out that testing
games is frankly really,

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.420
really hard.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:39.510
So recently, I
had surgery, which

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:43.370
is why I'm sort of limping
around on stage here.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:47.360
And I spent a lot
of time lying in bed

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:51.480
and did what might some might
call research, which involved

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:52.790
playing a whole bunch of games.

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:54.800
Now, I don't actually
play games that often.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:59.220
But in bed, I was able to
play quite a few games.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.040
I also, during the
playing of these games--

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.430
hence the research part--

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:08.780
was thinking a lot about
how to test these games

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:13.300
and how hard it is
to test these games.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:14.100
So why is it hard?

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:19.590
Well, firstly,
games are rendered

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.170
with something like OpenGL,
or one of the newer frameworks

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:27.560
like Vulkan, which makes
them really hard to test

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.070
because none of the
existing testing

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:33.860
frameworks that you might use
for testing applications will

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.475
work like UI Automator,
or EXI Test, or Espresso.

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:45.560
So these traditional
tools don't work.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:56.120
But also, games offer a huge
array of possible inputs.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.500
You can imagine playing a game
like Clash of Clans or World

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.170
of Warcraft.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:03.810
And you're able to move that
character pretty much anywhere

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.300
on the screen, or build
that tower in the corner,

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.070
or in the middle, or whatever.

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:11.970
So essentially, the
number of interactions

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.245
you can have with a game
is virtually infinite.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:23.880
And games are often
deliberately unpredictable,

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:28.610
which means that they give
a different experience

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.850
to the user every time.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:32.850
But that means, they give
a different experience

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:36.130
to the test every time,
which makes it very hard

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:37.600
to write an automated test.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:46.215
And lastly, issues
are harder to reach.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:52.230
Often, you have to get to
some of the later levels

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:56.920
to actually find a
particular issue.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:58.900
You can imagine that
your ogre on level

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.720
10, your favorite ogre,
has suddenly died,

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:02.890
and you have no idea why.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:14.010
Now up until quite recently, the
only generally available option

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:19.065
for testing mobile Apple
games was Monkey Testing.

00:08:19.065 --> 00:08:20.440
And if anybody
has had experience

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:23.220
with Monkey Testing,
especially of games,

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:26.350
you'll know it's not
particularly helpful.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:31.060
Rarely does it get
very far into the game

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:34.150
to be particularly useful.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:38.830
And also, it doesn't exercise
the game as a user would.

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:43.559
So you may not even care
about the issues it finds.

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:47.040
It's probably good at finding
crashes or issues at startup,

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.470
but really not much more.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:55.370
So before we get
into actually talking

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:59.510
about the solutions, the
specific solutions for testing

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:01.910
mobile games in
the Cloud, I need

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:07.630
to first introduce our Cloud
testing platform, which

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:10.845
is Firebase Test Lab.

00:09:10.845 --> 00:09:12.220
This is actually
something that I

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:17.090
have been working on at Google
for about five years now.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:21.470
Firebase Test Lab
is part of the suite

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:29.780
of application quality products
within the Firebase umbrella.

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:36.130
It allows you to test your
app or game in the Cloud

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:39.850
across a variety of
different devices,

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:42.940
including, most importantly,
some high-end gaming

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:52.450
devices on iOS, on Android,
on physical devices,

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:55.340
as well as virtual devices.

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:59.310
You can test in
different languages.

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:02.600
And you can test in different
screen orientations.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:09.750
And with the comprehensive
test results that you get,

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:14.365
you have screenshots and video
and then the most important

00:10:14.365 --> 00:10:16.410
part-- performance
metrics, as we

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:21.480
talked about earlier, such as
startup time, and specifically

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:25.210
for games frames per second, as
well as CPU, memory, network,

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:25.710
and so on.

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:34.710
Importantly, these metrics are
synchronized with the video,

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:40.350
so that you can tell when your
video is displaying, whatever's

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:41.677
happening in your game.

00:10:41.677 --> 00:10:44.010
You can tell what's happening
within performance metrics

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:44.950
and vise versa.

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:50.440
So you get some context into
whatever problem there is.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:53.070
Also, you get crashes, not
responding events, logs,

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:54.260
and much, much more.

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:02.130
Firebase Test Lab is also--

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:04.080
and this is
surprisingly unknown--

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:09.060
Firebase Test Lab is also
the engine behind the Play

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.560
Pre-launch Report.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:15.580
How many people here have heard
of the Play Pre-Launch Report?

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:16.210
How many?

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:21.140
1, 2-- wow, 2, OK.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:25.130
So in case you don't know, which
is apparently 99% of you hear,

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:26.870
what the Pre-Launch Report is--

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:31.010
Pre-Launch Report
is a free report

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:35.480
that we give to developers
for simply uploading your app

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:40.223
or game into a testing
track within the Play Store.

00:11:40.223 --> 00:11:42.140
It's under the Release
section within the Play

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:46.550
Store of your app
after you deploy it.

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:53.540
It tests your app or game
across about 10 devices

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:56.960
and gives you the same
comprehensive test results.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.750
And did I mention it's free?

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:05.120
So there's really no excuse not
to at least take a look at it.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:06.810
All right, so when
I mentioned I talked

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:09.680
to a lot of game
developers, the one thing

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:14.890
that I heard most often
from them is security.

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:16.810
We heard this from
so many of you--

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:22.990
that the security of the game is
your biggest concern, much more

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:26.400
so than, say,
application developers.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:29.400
That is because a leak
of a pre-production game

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.730
would be really, really bad.

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:38.400
And that is why we've put a lot
of effort, put a lot of focus

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:45.220
around making sure that the
device on which you're testing

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:49.390
is completely cleaned up both
before and after your test.

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:52.960
So there's no possibility
of leaking the game

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:57.770
or seeing the results of
the game in a future test.

00:12:57.770 --> 00:13:01.400
In most cases, we simply flash
the device between test runs.

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:07.550
We also perform a security check
on the submitter of the test,

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:09.520
as well as the
game or app itself.

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:13.942
And there you go.

00:13:13.942 --> 00:13:15.150
I knew I was going to forget.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:19.140
All right, so how
much does this cost?

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:21.060
I hear you ask.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:28.810
Well, we charge you $1 an hour
for an Android virtual device,

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:33.460
and then $5 an hour for
a physical device, iOS

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:36.210
or Android.

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:40.230
But the key here is, we
only charge you for as long

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:46.410
as your test runs, which
means that if your test runs

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:51.360
for, say, five
minutes on a Pixel 2,

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:55.050
it'll cost you about $0.40.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:56.520
We don't charge
you for how long it

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:59.540
takes to install
your application,

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:01.520
to pull off the results
from the device,

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:03.830
to post-process those
results, to ship them off

00:14:03.830 --> 00:14:06.110
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:11.160
We only charge you for how
long you specify your test run.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:14.310
And that's pretty
cheap, trust me.

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:17.960
So really, there's no
excuse not to implement

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:22.175
some regimen of automated
testing, which brings us

00:14:22.175 --> 00:14:24.800
to how you might go about doing
that-- which is what you've all

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.000
been patiently waiting for
me to start talking about.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:32.730
All right, so firstly
and most importantly,

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:37.850
you're going to want to
prepare your game for testing

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:40.640
in the Cloud.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:45.320
You may want to create a
special version of your game,

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:49.220
or you can build test ability
actually into the code.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:52.670
In Test Lab, we set a property
that you can test for.

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:56.560
You can then bracket
the code in your game

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:58.407
by testing with
an "if statement"

00:14:58.407 --> 00:14:59.740
that simply tests this property.

00:15:02.755 --> 00:15:04.130
So there are many
things that you

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:06.950
might want to do to make
your game more testable.

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:10.650
Firstly, you can
focus on the preamble.

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:14.170
You could remove some
or all of the preamble,

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:19.180
so that the tests can get
to the gameplay more easily.

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:21.020
You could remove some
of the log in part,

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:24.990
or the registration,
or something.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:27.090
As I talked about
earlier, you probably

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:29.790
want to remove the
randomness, so that the tests

00:15:29.790 --> 00:15:32.340
that you write are less flaky.

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:35.880
They see the same
board, or game,

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:39.450
or they can interact in
the same way every time.

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:46.100
You might want to start the
game at a specific level--

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:49.220
or load a whole bunch
of assets into the game,

00:15:49.220 --> 00:15:52.930
so that the game looks like it's
been played for a long time.

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:54.940
That will allow you
to more easily test

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:59.200
these later levels or more
sophisticated gameplay.

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:00.940
And of course,
there's a lot probably

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:04.360
a lot more that you can do that
may be specific to the game.

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:13.120
So with Test Lab,
you can run lots

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:16.480
of different kinds of
tests, some of which

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:22.180
are specifically
targeted at games.

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:25.570
One test that requires
little-- well really,

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:28.120
no effort on your part--

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.360
is Robo.

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:32.670
Anybody familiar with Robo here?

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:35.010
Yes, all right, we got two.

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:45.050
OK, Robo is a framework
of application crawlers

00:16:45.050 --> 00:16:49.390
that are used under
different conditions.

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:56.700
So when Robo sees an opaque
screen, such as that rendered

00:16:56.700 --> 00:17:02.540
by, say, OpenGL, or
Vulkan, or whatever,

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:07.069
it will switch to a crawler
called the Enhanced Monkey

00:17:07.069 --> 00:17:07.760
Action Crawler.

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:15.940
This crawler today
uses machine learning

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:20.290
to interpret an opaque
screen and identify screen

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:22.690
elements on which it can click.

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:30.870
Now, Robo shows a yellow
circle in the video, which

00:17:30.870 --> 00:17:34.950
you can see behind me whenever
it interacts or clicks

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:36.960
with your app, the app or game.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:43.810
And clicking, as it turns
