00:22:00.450 --> 00:22:05.600
Let's switch back to
the slides please.

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:17.450
With a little bit more
effort, you can actually

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:19.010
record test scripts.

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:26.130
So last year at the Games
Development Conference

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:31.210
in San Francisco, we announced
a partnership with NetEase.

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:33.240
NetEase is one of the
largest games development

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:34.290
companies in China.

00:22:37.700 --> 00:22:41.810
They had previously built
an integrated development

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:47.570
environment called Air Test,
which they used internally

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:50.900
for testing their own games.

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:52.980
So at the Games
Department Conference,

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:56.970
they released that development
environment to the world.

00:22:56.970 --> 00:23:03.810
They also open sourced a couple
of different testing frameworks

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:07.250
that they use for
testing their own games.

00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:10.520
The first is called
Air Test, like the IDE

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:12.840
and involves image recognition.

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:14.510
So you can simply
select an image

00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:16.520
that you wanted to
recognize, and then

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:20.170
interact with that
particular screen element.

00:23:20.170 --> 00:23:26.540
The second, and probably more
importantly, is called Poco.

00:23:26.540 --> 00:23:32.090
Poco is actually built
into the game engines.

00:23:32.090 --> 00:23:34.790
So it actually
publishes a hierarchy

00:23:34.790 --> 00:23:38.600
of screen elements, which
allows you to interact directly

00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:41.000
with the screen elements
from within the IDE

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:42.650
and from within
your test scripts.

00:23:42.650 --> 00:23:50.460
So Poco has support for
Unity, and Cocos, and also

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:53.340
the Android framework itself.

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:57.580
So you can record scripts
with a game running locally.

00:23:57.580 --> 00:24:00.700
I'll show that in a second.

00:24:00.700 --> 00:24:04.680
You can then package those
scripts into an instrumentation

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:08.790
test that you then
are able to run

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:13.450
across a variety of different
devices in Test Lab.

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:15.450
And the great thing about
all these test scripts

00:24:15.450 --> 00:24:21.570
is, it's incredibly easy to
go back in and rerecord them

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:25.230
if, for some reason, your
UI or your images change.

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:32.060
Let's switch to the demo.

00:24:32.060 --> 00:24:34.340
Marvin, please.

00:24:34.340 --> 00:24:37.690
So this is Air Test.

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:40.830
This is the Integrated
Development Environment.

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:43.740
And I have Mecha
Hamster, which is

00:24:43.740 --> 00:24:45.750
the game I mentioned
earlier, running locally

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:47.900
on this device here.

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:50.788
I probably shouldn't pick it up.

00:24:50.788 --> 00:24:52.330
So what I'm going
to do is, I'm going

00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:55.180
to show you how easy it
is to build up a script.

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:57.850
So I can, for
instance, say, I want

00:24:57.850 --> 00:25:00.850
to touch this area
of the screen.

00:25:00.850 --> 00:25:03.970
And we're going to
use Air Test here.

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:07.150
And it will now attempt
to recognize that image

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:09.930
and click on it.

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:12.350
And then, I can
say, well, I want

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:17.590
to wait for something to appear.

00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:21.390
So I'll select
another part here.

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:25.100
So as you can see, I'm
building up a script

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:29.560
by interacting with my
game that I can then

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:34.090
test across this
device running locally

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:37.420
or under this
strangely-named menu

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:40.400
item called Overseas Devices.

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:45.690
I can build this into
an instrumentation test

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:48.210
that I can then run
across all those devices

00:25:48.210 --> 00:25:51.750
that I want to run them on
inside of Test Lab-- which

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:54.570
gives you the ability to
write these test scripts

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:57.180
and then test them on the
devices your users have.

00:26:00.777 --> 00:26:02.610
Marvin, if we can switch
back to the slides.

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:11.530
And if you want to learn more--

00:26:11.530 --> 00:26:14.520
I'll step out the
way, just in case--

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:16.070
these are some links.

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:19.690
There's the GitHub project,
where Air Test resides.

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:25.467
And that's the link for the
way you can download the IDE.

00:26:25.467 --> 00:26:26.550
I'll let you take a photo.

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:27.050
There we go.

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:35.500
So the third option that
I'm going to present today

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:37.790
is called Game Loops.

00:26:37.790 --> 00:26:41.210
We introduced Game Loops
probably a year and a half

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:41.910
or so ago.

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:51.510
Game Loops provide a
way for you to define

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:58.500
one or more critical user
journeys through your game.

00:26:58.500 --> 00:27:01.310
So you could, for
instance, focus

00:27:01.310 --> 00:27:04.340
and have a critical user
journey, a Game Loop,

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:07.910
for every level in your game.

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:11.870
Or you can focus on every
character in your game

00:27:11.870 --> 00:27:14.150
and write a Game Loop for that.

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:19.200
You can have any number
of these Game Loops

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:22.910
and built into your game.

00:27:22.910 --> 00:27:26.850
So Mecha Hamster, which
you can see behind me,

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:28.860
is an open source
game that you can

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:36.790
find on GitHub, which we built
to test out many, if not all,

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:39.160
of the features of Firebase.

00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:40.840
So it has real-time database.

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:42.580
It has auth support.

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:48.790
It uses lots of the different
tools within Firebase.

00:27:48.790 --> 00:27:52.350
But it also has a Game
Loop built into it.

00:27:57.060 --> 00:28:00.300
So I'm going to quickly demo
how we built this Game Loop.

00:28:00.300 --> 00:28:05.810
Marvin, if we can switch
again to the demo.

00:28:05.810 --> 00:28:08.840
So I may not have mentioned
this, but Mecha Hamster

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:13.100
itself is a Unity game, which
means we built it within Unity.

00:28:17.370 --> 00:28:22.857
And I'm going to talk a little
bit about how we did that.

00:28:22.857 --> 00:28:24.440
So I'm going to put
up some code here.

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:29.830
So the first most important
thing about Game Loops

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:32.050
is that they need
to be declared.

00:28:32.050 --> 00:28:34.270
They need to be
declared, in this case,

00:28:34.270 --> 00:28:35.790
in the Android manifest.

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:38.500
And you can see a
number of lines here

00:28:38.500 --> 00:28:40.000
that's important to highlight.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:44.140
So you need this part
here basically talking

00:28:44.140 --> 00:28:49.800
about setting up the
metadata for the test Loop.

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:54.230
For every test Loop,
need an intent filter

00:28:54.230 --> 00:28:56.340
that declares the test Loop.

00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:58.330
And that's down there.

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:00.100
So now, you've set
up and declared these

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:02.880
within your Android manifest.

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:09.007
The way we built Game
Loops in Mecha Hamster--

00:29:09.007 --> 00:29:11.340
and this is not the only way
you could build Game Loops,

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:13.890
but it is the gateway we
built within Mecha Hamster

00:29:13.890 --> 00:29:18.080
and it's particularly easy
to set up within Unity--

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:22.090
is, we have a special
mode of the game that

00:29:22.090 --> 00:29:25.450
records all of the
user interactions

00:29:25.450 --> 00:29:29.260
or the events with
the game into a file.

00:29:29.260 --> 00:29:33.610
So this Gameplay Recording
Enabled equals False.

00:29:33.610 --> 00:29:35.610
When we set that to
true, Mecha Hamster

00:29:35.610 --> 00:29:38.810
will start recording
events into a file.

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:43.650
We can then take those
events and load them

00:29:43.650 --> 00:29:47.420
in as an asset within the game.

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:49.310
And I'll show that
a little bit here.

00:29:49.310 --> 00:29:52.580
And then when the game
starts up and it's asked--

00:29:52.580 --> 00:29:55.790
this is actually the
Gameplay Recorder Class

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:58.160
that is recorded that
basically records the events

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:02.060
and puts them into a
file, serializes them out.

00:30:02.060 --> 00:30:07.810
When the game starts
up, you can test

00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:11.040
whether you're running
in a Game Loop,

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:14.970
and then load those assets,
and simply replay these events.

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:16.553
Now, as I say, that's
not the only way

00:30:16.553 --> 00:30:17.637
you can build a Game Loop.

00:30:17.637 --> 00:30:19.817
You could build out a code
if you wished just simply

00:30:19.817 --> 00:30:21.150
calling function after function.

00:30:21.150 --> 00:30:23.340
But this is a pretty
easy way to do that.

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:31.280
Marvin, if we can switch
back please to the slides.

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:39.490
And if you want to know
more about Game Loops

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:42.810
and how you might
build Game Loops,

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:44.000
there's a rather long link.

00:30:49.590 --> 00:30:57.710
So I've presented three separate
automated testing mechanisms.

00:30:57.710 --> 00:31:03.300
So let's talk a
little bit about how

00:31:03.300 --> 00:31:05.580
you might use each of
these different testing--

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:09.370
how and when you might use
each of these different testing

00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:10.160
mechanisms.

00:31:10.160 --> 00:31:13.980
So firstly, I talked about Robo.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:19.930
So Robo is really good
for testing the preamble

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:20.560
to the game.

00:31:20.560 --> 00:31:24.250
So it follows through the menu
items, logging in, registering,

00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:25.940
all of that kind of stuff.

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:30.260
It's also really good if you
don't have any tests yet.

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:33.448
So maybe you're starting
off with an initial version

00:31:33.448 --> 00:31:33.990
of your game.

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:35.270
You haven't written any tests.

00:31:35.270 --> 00:31:36.920
You just want to test it--

00:31:36.920 --> 00:31:41.280
first internal version
that you want to play with.

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:45.150
What we see application
developers doing with Test Lab

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:52.130
is using Robo as a last
ditch smoke test signal

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:57.760
before you push to production,
so that you can make

00:31:57.760 --> 00:32:01.570
sure by looking at the test
results, looking for crashes,

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:04.000
not responding,
whatever, looking

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:06.100
at the screenshots,
the video, makes sure

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:08.430
that you've not done
anything stupid.

00:32:12.910 --> 00:32:23.020
Air Test, what I
presented second, good

00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:26.100
for the UI navigation.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:29.110
It allows you to test
basic gameplay, allowing

00:32:29.110 --> 00:32:30.820
you to interact with the game.

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:38.040
Game Loops are really good
for testing the critical user

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:41.060
journeys through your game.

00:32:41.060 --> 00:32:44.000
And because it's the
same critical user

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:47.450
journey each time, it's
really good for catching

00:32:47.450 --> 00:32:49.680
performance regressions.

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:54.610
So you can show that
your performance is not

00:32:54.610 --> 00:32:56.350
degraded in between releases.

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:58.510
And you can look back
over the previous releases

00:32:58.510 --> 00:33:00.400
and see how long did
this critical user

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:03.240
journey take to run previously.

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:06.120
And lastly, of course,
we're not trying

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:11.270
to take over all the manual
testing-- at least not yet.

00:33:11.270 --> 00:33:13.820
Manual testing is
still really useful

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:19.820
for complex interactions,
such as multiplayer, or indeed

00:33:19.820 --> 00:33:22.085
some of the late-level testing.

00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:29.640
So as I always like
to say, now, with all

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:35.650
of those tests that you've
written, all you need to do

00:33:35.650 --> 00:33:38.840
is upload them into a
continuous integration system.

00:33:38.840 --> 00:33:41.740
And every time
you make a change,

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:44.350
you can be much more confident--
we talk about the safety

00:33:44.350 --> 00:33:47.710
net here-- you can be much
more confident that you did not

00:33:47.710 --> 00:33:52.848
break the game in some
obscure way, which means

00:33:52.848 --> 00:33:54.140
that your developers are happy.

00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:55.490
They have that safety net.

00:33:55.490 --> 00:33:59.270
They know that for the most
part, every change they commit,

00:33:59.270 --> 00:34:00.770
they can tell if
it's going to break

00:34:00.770 --> 00:34:03.140
the game in some
fundamental way.

00:34:03.140 --> 00:34:04.620
So they're happy.

00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:06.680
Your users are happy
because they're not

00:34:06.680 --> 00:34:09.770
seeing the kind of issues
that you're catching

00:34:09.770 --> 00:34:12.350
with all of these tests--

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:14.389
which is, of course,
ultimately what we want.

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:26.500
So in summary,
obviously, I hope you

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:29.090
take advantage of the testing
tools that I presented today.

00:34:29.090 --> 00:34:32.790
It's like a given.

00:34:32.790 --> 00:34:36.960
These tools allow you to test
with Test Lab across a variety

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:39.060
of different devices,
the sort of devices

00:34:39.060 --> 00:34:41.144
that your users are using today.

00:34:43.739 --> 00:34:48.320
But I also want to talk a
little bit about the future

00:34:48.320 --> 00:34:55.580
because, as we've heard from
so many of you, what you really

00:34:55.580 --> 00:35:01.880
need is for us to be able to
test your game as your best

00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:05.770
manual tester can.

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:12.800
Now, that's a hard but
exciting destination to reach.

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:16.490
And we hope to see you there.

00:35:16.490 --> 00:35:19.630
In the meantime, I wish you luck
building the best game ever.

00:35:19.630 --> 00:35:22.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

